In `/Users/steven/pythons/scan-to-csv/doc-source-enriched.py:679–685`, it:

1. Collects every path belonging to a content-hash group.
2. Joins them into one large `duplicate_paths` string.
3. Writes that same string into every row in the group.

That makes the duplicate-path output grow quadratically with group size: a large group produces a large path list, repeated many times. Common identical files—especially in dependency, copied-project, or generated trees—can therefore make the CSV enormous without representing an equally large amount of useful metadata.

What we actually observed

During the earlier partial inspection:
- A single `duplicate_paths` field reached 336,390 characters.
- The file was still changing during the read.
- Its size increased from 712,684,327 to 726,048,214 bytes across that inspection.

Those were intermediate observations, not final counts. My latest size check was blocked by the tool, so I cannot independently confirm its current size above a gigabyte.

Two implementation choices compound the problem

• The output is written directly to the final CSV.

At `doc-source-enriched.py:634–658`, it opens the destination with `&quot;w&quot;` and writes rows sequentially. There is no temporary-file-and-rename completion boundary. That is why we could inspect a partially written, continually growing `iterm2-meta.csv`.

• The path-redaction change loses identity without eliminating repetition.

At `doc-source-enriched.py:639–656`, paths are made relative to each file’s containing directory—not the original scan root. Our fixture proved that two different files could both export as:

```text
original_path:   root:shared
full_path:       same.md
duplicate_paths: same.md|same.md
```

So the CSV is not merely oversized: its default path presentation can make it ambiguous. That was a defect in the earlier repair I made, and the existing tests did not catch it.

The correct design

Keep one row per file with:

```text
root_id
relative_path
content_hash
duplicate_group_id
```

Then store group membership once in a separate table:

```text
duplicate_group_id
root_id
relative_path
```

