ose commands can produce millions of characters and hide the signal. Start with bounded summaries, then drill into the roots that matter.\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Load local project guidance first**\n   - Read applicable AGENTS.md / project skill guidance before classifying files.\n   - If a domain-specific project skill exists, load it before interpreting directories.\n\n2. **Quantify git state without flooding output**\n   - Use porcelain status counts, top changed roots, and shortstats.\n   - Distinguish staged changes from unstaged changes and untracked material.\n   - Treat huge staged imports as a first-class risk even if the repo&#39;s prior snapshot said \&quot;clean\&quot;.\n\n3. **Map top-level ownership**\n   - Identify authoring source, references/vendor, generated/cache/build/runtime material, docs/research, and product subprojects.\n   - Prefer small scripted counters over recursive terminal output.\n   - Use `eza` for human-readable directory listings when available, but keep depth bounded.\n\n4. **Inspect representative files**\n   - Read manifests, READMEs, core scripts, and existing reviews before forming conclusions.\n   - For imported apps, inspect package/project manifests and validation scripts instead of assuming toolchains.\n\n5. **Recover prior automation artifacts before recreating them.**\n   - When the user asks \&quot;what script did we use?\&quot; or supplies a prior transcript/path, search conversation history first, then verify the referenced filesystem paths.\n   - Separate three layers: the original source script, generated manifests/changelogs, and cached compiled artifacts such as `__pycache__/*.pyc`. Treat the `.py` source as the editable/runnable authority; a matching `.pyc` is evidence of prior execution, not a separate implementation.\n   - Distinguish extension restoration from semantic/content-aware naming. A MIME/signature detector such as `restore_chatgpt_dat.py` can safely turn `.dat` into `.png`, `.mp3`, `.mp4`, etc.; it does not infer names such as `BirdOfBlue.png`. Do not conflate these stages.\n   - Verify recovered scripts with `python3 -m py_compile` and `--help` before recommending them. Check manifests/changelogs for the actual rename run and preserve their old-to-new mapping.\n   - If a prior conversation describes a script but no saved source path exists, report it as transcript-only rather than claiming it was created. Do not silently recreate or overwrite it while merely investigating.\n\n6. **Run safe validation only**\n   - Compile/lint scripts where appropriate.\n   - Run read-only doctors and project-specific list commands.\n   - Do not build, install, delete, commit, or clean caches unless explicitly asked.\n\n7. **Report boundaries and risks first**\n   - Lead with the repo&#39;s actual shape and current git risk.\n   - Separate \&quot;pipeline appears healthy\&quot; from \&quot;repository hygiene is risky\&quot;.\n   - Recommend triage before commits when staged imports dominate the diff.\n\n## Bounded dirty-worktree probe\n\nUse the recipe in `references/dirty-worktree-triage.md` before any repo-wide review conclusion when the tree might be large or dirty.\n\n## Package-inventory and environment evidence\n\nWhen a user pastes a long `pip list` or package inventory while investigating a tool:\n\n1. Treat it as evidence, not as a verified single environment. Mixed Python versions, editable installs, repeated rows, malformed wrapped lines, and truncated versions are common.\n2. Stop echoing each package once the capability decision is clear. Summarize only packages relevant to the task, record malformed entries as validation gaps, and move to an executable probe.\n3. Validate the exact interpreter that will run the artifact with focused imports and `python -m pip show`/`python -c` checks. Do not infer that a package is usable merely because it appears in a pasted inventory.\n4. Separate extension restoration from semantic/content-aware naming: magic/MIME detection can safely restore `.dat` extensions, while image naming requires visual review or a vision-capable labeling step. Do not claim the former performs the latter.\n5. For large rename jobs, preserve an old→new manifest, use dry-run first, handle collisions deterministically, and verify source/destination state after execution.\n\n## Pitfalls\n\n- Do not use full `git status` as the first probe in a repo with possible archive explosions or generated data.\n- Do not mistake generated data volume for authored source volume.\n- Do not treat a source-tree import, downloaded archive expansion, or cache directory as canonical just because it is present.\n- Do not commit or recommend committing a massive staged import without first naming the affected roots and likely ownership classes.\n- Do not keep producing package-by-package acknowledgements after the relevant capability set is established.\n- Do not conflate a saved extension-restoration script with a missing or unverified semantic renamer.\n- Do not save one-off repo narratives in skills; save only reusable investigation procedure and durable probes.\n&quot;, &quot;path&quot;: &quot;software-development/repo-forensics/SKILL.md&quot;, &quot;skill_dir&quot;: &quot;/Users/steven/.hermes/skills/software-development/repo-forensics&quot;, &quot;org_provenance&quot;: null, &quot;linked_files&quot;: {&quot;references&quot;: [&quot;references/dirty-worktree-triage.md&quot;, &quot;references/runtime-executable-audit.md&quot;]}, &quot;usage_hint&quot;: &quot;To view linked files, call skill_view(name, file_path) where file_path is e.g. &#39;references/api.md&#39; or &#39;assets/config.yaml&#39;&quot;, &quot;required_environment_variables&quot;: [], &quot;required_commands&quot;: [], &quot;missing_required_environment_variables&quot;: [], &quot;missing_credential_files&quot;: [], &quot;missing_required_commands&quot;: [], &quot;setup_needed&quot;: false, &quot;setup_skipped&quot;: false, &quot;readiness_status&quot;: &quot;available&quot;, &quot;_source_path&quot;: &quot;/Users/steven/.hermes/skills/software-development/repo-forensics/SKILL.md&quot;, &quot;metadata&quot;: {&quot;hermes&quot;: {&quot;tags&quot;: [&quot;repository&quot;, &quot;git&quot;, &quot;audit&quot;, &quot;inspection&quot;, &quot;dirty-worktree&quot;, &quot;monorepo&quot;], &quot;related_skills&quot;: [&quot;codebase-inspection&quot;]}}}</code></pre></div>  </div></div>
