<div class="message message-tool active">  <div class="message-header">    <div class="role-badge"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="chevron lucide lucide-chevron-right"><path d="m9 18 6-6-6-6"/></svg> <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-terminal"><polyline points="4 17 10 11 4 5"/><line x1="12" x2="20" y1="19" y2="19"/></svg> tool</div>    <div class="timestamp">2026-09-05 00:34:15</div>  </div>  <div class="message-body">  <div class="content"><pre><code>{&quot;success&quot;: true, &quot;name&quot;: &quot;metadata-first-home-auditing&quot;, &quot;description&quot;: &quot;Use for complete privacy-bounded home metadata indexes.&quot;, &quot;tags&quot;: [&quot;home-audit&quot;, &quot;metadata&quot;, &quot;filesystem&quot;, &quot;lineage&quot;, &quot;privacy&quot;, &quot;inventory&quot;], &quot;related_skills&quot;: [], &quot;content&quot;: &quot;---\nname: metadata-first-home-auditing\ndescription: \&quot;Use for complete privacy-bounded home metadata indexes.\&quot;\nversion: 1.0.0\nauthor: Hermes\nlicense: MIT\nplatforms: [macos, linux]\nmetadata:\n  hermes:\n    tags: [home-audit, metadata, filesystem, lineage, privacy, inventory]\n    category: devops\n---\n\n# Metadata-First Home Auditing\n\nUse this skill when a user asks to audit, index, inventory, or explore an entire home directory, including hidden folders and runtime trees, while preserving privacy and source lineage.\n\nThis is a metadata and coverage workflow. It does not turn path names into semantic truth, import private histories, perform cleanup, or claim that a capability is active because a file exists.\n\n## Core distinction\n\nA complete filesystem index and a useful semantic discovery index are different artifacts:\n\n```text\ncomplete recursive metadata manifest\n  → proves path-level coverage and filesystem accounting\n\nbounded semantic discovery index\n  → supports interpretation of approved authored roots\n\nprimary-source audit\n  → validates purpose, lineage, current role, and runtime claims\n```\n\nNever collapse these into one inventory.\n\n## Safety boundary\n\nDefault to read-only behavior. Do not read or print secret values, credentials, tokens, cookies, browser histories, private conversation bodies, private databases, or authentication stores. Do not follow symlinks. Do not execute discovered files. Do not delete, move, deduplicate, synchronize, upload, publish, or install anything as part of an audit.\n\nSensitive-looking names are path-level triage signals only. They must not be treated as proof that a file contains a secret.\n\n## Two-pass procedure\n\n### Pass A — complete metadata coverage\n\nUse when the user explicitly asks for everything under `~/` or another complete boundary.\n\nRecord for every reachable entry:\n\n- absolute and relative path;\n- file, directory, symlink, or other kind;\n- hidden-component flag;\n- sensitive-name flag;\n- size where available;\n- modification time;\n- child count for directories;\n- filesystem error if encountered.\n\nInclude dotfolders, caches, dependencies, archives, runtime trees, and system user directories in this pass. Do not read their contents. Keep the manifest streamable and compressed when large. For very large trees, use a tracked background process rather than a foreground timeout.\n\n### Pass B — bounded semantic discovery\n\nUse approved authored roots and primary documents to build a smaller, interpretable index. Prune high-volume generated trees, dependency directories, VCS internals, caches, virtual environments, browser data, and private runtime stores unless a separate explicit approval exists.\n\nCapture provisional role, evidence state, concept-current, lineage relation, and smallest next gate. Read the original README, manifest, source entrypoint, test, or handoff rather than relying on generated summaries.\n\n## Evidence states\n\nUse:\n\n- `observed`: directly measured or read;\n- `declared`: asserted by a source or manifest;\n- `inherited`: carried by history, backup, export, or prior report;\n- `inferred`: provisional relation;\n- `possible`: future-facing hypothesis;\n- `unknown`: needs another inquiry;\n- `blocked`: cannot be verified under current boundaries;\n- `runtime-proven`: only after a bounded test with persisted evidence.\n\nPath existence, count, timestamp, size, or hash alone cannot establish purpose, ownership, canonicality, duplication, activation, safety, licensing, or readiness.\n\n## Completion and checkpoint discipline\n\nFor a complete pass, produce:\n\n- compressed recursive manifest;\n- JSON summary with counts and scope;\n- readable coverage report;\n- categorized semantic index, if semantic discovery was requested;\n- explicit exclusions and prune roots;\n- filesystem error count and sample;\n- sensitive-name flag count without values.\n\nIf a run times out, leaves an incomplete archive, or terminates before the summary is written, label that artifact as an incomplete checkpoint. Do not use it as the authoritative manifest. Rerun in a tracked background process or use a resumable per-root strategy.\n\nValidate the final output with a streaming parser. Confirm that the parsed record count equals the summary count, headers are correct, malformed records are zero, and gzip/archive integrity is complete.\n\n## Lineage rules\n\nDo not call two paths duplicates from names, locations, sizes, or similar counts. Backups, mirrors, projections, compatibility layers, archives, generated copies, and alternate host renderings may have different roles. Record relations such as `candidate-source`, `projection`, `backup`, `runtime`, `history-shaped`, `generated`, or `unknown` until primary evidence resolves them.\n\nA large subtree is a question, not a finding. A directory named `agent`, `product`, `memory`, `archive`, or `cache` is a provisional lead, not a verified role.\n\n## Product and ecosystem audits\n\nWhen the home audit is being used to identify reusable products or offers:\n\n1. use path metadata only to discover currents;\n2. select a few primary authored roots;\n3. read source documents and implementation/test surfaces;\n4. compare declared claims with current proof;\n5. preserve privacy, license, and ownership uncertainty;\n6. propose only after evidence is assembled;\n7. state the smallest customer-safe proof rather than a broad product promise.\n\n## Required verification\n\nAt minimum:\n\n```text\nroot exists\ncomplete manifest parses\nsummary count matches parsed records\nno filesystem errors hidden by the script\nsensitive values were not read or printed\nsemantic index headers and row widths are valid\nreport names limitations and incomplete checkpoints\n```\n\nA reusable implementation pattern and validation checklist are in `references/complete-home-manifest.md`.\n\n## Pitfalls\n\n- Treating a bounded scan as “everything.”\n- Treating a full metadata manifest as semantic understanding.\n- Pruning caches/runtime trees without telling the user when they explicitly asked for complete coverage.\n- Hashing or reading all files during a metadata-only pass.\n- Letting a timeout-produced compressed file masquerade as a complete index.\n- Following symlinks and accidentally escaping the approved boundary.\n- Printing sensitive path contents while reporting flags.\n- Calling backups or mirrors duplicates before lineage review.\n- Converting path counts into product readiness or market demand.\n- Building a dashboard before the manifest and evidence contracts are validated.\n&quot;, &quot;path&quot;: &quot;devops/metadata-first-home-auditing/SKILL.md&quot;, &quot;skill_dir&quot;: &quot;/Users/steven/.hermes/skills/devops/metadata-first-home-auditing&quot;, &quot